        "original_id": "dde2d281-f8a4-50ea-8a4e-4712eb449a8b",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Products: Ghosts (2006 film), Battle for Haditha\n- Concepts: Gweilo\n- People: Nick Broomfield\n- Topics: Cantonese slang, racial terminology\n\nFacts:\n- Ghosts (2006 film) takes its title from the Cantonese slang term \"Gweilo\" (鬼佬), which means \"ghost man\" and is used for white people.\n- In its unmodified form, Gweilo applies only to people of European ethnicities and has a history of racially deprecatory use.\n- Battle for Haditha is Nick Broomfield's follow-up to Ghosts.\n- Battle for Haditha dramatizes real events using a documentary style.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "18ac892d-1ddb-50f2-914b-ba6dabc3b492",
        "text": "Page: Ghosts (2006 film). Sentence: The title is a reference to the Cantonese slang term \"Gweilo\" (鬼佬), meaning \"ghost man\", used for white people.\nPage: Gweilo. Sentence: In its unmodified form, it applies only to European ethnicities and has a history of racially deprecatory use.\nPage: Battle for Haditha. Sentence: Dramatising real events using a documentary style, \"Battle for Haditha\" is Broomfield's follow up to \"Ghosts.\"",
